    "output": "Done. The goal is met.\n\n## What I inspected first\n\n| Module | Provides | Impact of the change |\n|---|---|---|\n| `app/calc.py` | `add(a, b)` (only) | **Target of the change** \u2014 `subtract` was missing |\n| `app/text.py` | `normalize(value)` | Unrelated; no imports from `calc`, untouched |\n| `app/units.py` | `GRAMS_PER_KILOGRAM`, `to_grams`, `total_grams` | Builds on `calc` via `from app.calc import add` \u2014 it binds only `add` at import time |\n\nConclusion before editing: `app/units.py` only depends on `add`, so an **additive** change to `calc.py` (appending a new function, leaving `add` byte-identical) could not break it. Changing `add`'s signature/semantics or renaming anything would have broken `total_grams` \u2192 `add(*kilograms)`. So the change was kept purely additive.\n\n## The change (`app/calc.py`)\n\n```python\ndef subtract(a, b):\n    \"\"\"Return the difference of two numbers.\"\"\"\n    return a - b\n```\n\n`git diff` confirms this is the only modification in the repo: 5 added lines, zero deletions \u2014 `add`, `normalize`, `to_grams`, `total_grams` and `GRAMS_PER_KILOGRAM` are untouched.\n\n## Verification\n\n- **Baseline (before):** collection `ImportError: cannot import name 'subtract' from 'app.calc'` \u2014 suite failed with exit code 2.\n- **After:** public suite output was `.... 4 passed in 0.01s`, exit code 0. All four tests pass: `test_add`, `test_subtract` (`subtract(9, 4) == 5`, `subtract(0, 0) == 0`), `test_normalize`, and `test_units` (which transitively exercises `add` through `total_grams`).\n\nNo delegation was needed \u2014 the task was small and entirely dependent on reading three short files, so splitting it would have added coordination cost without parallelism.",
